'''II.''' ''intrans.'' '''1.''' To make preparations; carry out beforehand such negotiations or make such disposition in regard to some matter as may be necessary: as, to ''arrange'' about a passport, or for supplies; ''arrange'' with a publisher.—'''2.''' To come to an agreement or understanding in regard to something; make a settlement.
{{smaller block|We cannot ''arrange'' with our enemy in this conjuncture, without abandoning the interest of mankind.
{{right|''Burke'', A Regicide Peace.}}}}

'''arrangeable''' (a̤-rān′ja̤-bl), ''a.'' [< ''arrange'' + ''-able''.] Capable of being arranged.
{{smaller block|Fishes have crania made up of bones that are no more clearly ''arrangeable'' into segments like vertebræ than are the cranial bones of the highest mammal.
{{right|''H. Spencer'', Prin. of Biol., § 210.}}}}

'''arrangement''' (a̤-rānj′ment), ''n.'' [< F. ''arrangement'': see ''arrange'' and ''-ment''.] '''1.''' The act of arranging or putting in proper order; the state of being put in order; disposition in suitable form. {{smaller|Specifically, in the ''fine arts'', the combining of parts in a manner conformable to the character and aim of the design; composition.}}
{{smaller block|The freedom of syntactical ''arrangement'' which was possessed by the Anglo-Saxon is irrecoverably gone.
{{right|''G. P. Marsh'', Origin of Eng. Lang., p. 111.}}}}
'''2.''' That which is disposed in order; a system of parts disposed in due order; any combination of parts or materials.
{{smaller block|The interest of that portion of social ''arrangement'' is in the hands of all those who compose it.
{{right|''Burke.''}}}}
'''3.''' The style or mode in which things are arranged.
